  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nSYNOPTIC:\n\nA complex split-flow regime was found aloft over the North \nAmerican continent early this afternoon. A cutoff low was \npositioned off the coast of California with a broad relatively \nflat ridge farther east across the Canadian Prairies into \nOntario. A northern jet stream with several troughs stretched \nacross Canada. The first trough axis of note upstream from the \nNorthland stretched from northwest Manitoba to southern Alberta.\nA southern jet stream was found over the southwest US along the\nsouthern periphery of the cutoff low. An area of low pressure \nahead of the northern stream trough was centered over northern \nManitoba with a cool front trailing to the southwest over \nsoutheast Saskatchewan into eastern Montana. Surface high \npressure was situated over the Great Lakes and extended south \ninto the Gulf Coast. \n\nLATE THIS AFTERNOON THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT:\n\nThe northern stream trough propagates eastward across the \nCanadian Prairies and into northwest Ontario by 12Z Wednesday \nmorning. The surface low pressure and cold front will progress \neastward across the Northland Wednesday. Winds will turn \nnortherly and remain relatively light, around 5-10 mph. \nTemperatures will trend a few degrees cooler over the \nBorderlands, low 60s, while areas south of a line from Cass Lake\nto Duluth to Hurley will see temperatures in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s.\n\nTransient surface high pressure will drift eastward across the \nregion Wednesday night and Thursday as an upper-level ridge \nbuilds over the northern Plains and Canadian Prairies. \nTemperatures will remain well above normal as winds turn \nsoutherly. Lows Thursday morning will range from the low 30s to \naround 40 degrees. Look for highs in the low 60s to low 70s, \nabout 10-20 degrees above normal. Temps will be cooler for \nportions of the North Shore and Arrowhead due to on-shore flow. \nThose readings will be 5-10 degrees above normal with highs in \nthe middle 50s to low 60s.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT:\n\nThe Pacific cutoff low will get a slight nudge eastward and is \nexpected to move into the Intermountain West by Friday morning. \nSurface low pressure is forecast to develop in the Rockies and \nlift northeastward into Saskatchewan and Manitoba by Saturday \nmorning. A warm front is forecast to lift northward across the \nNorthland for the end of the work week. Isentropic ascent in the\nwarm sector will support a chance of rain showers Friday \nmorning over central and north-central Minnesota and spreading \neastward across the Northland through Saturday morning. The \nhighest rain chances will be found near and north of the warm \nfront, mainly over the Iron Range and Borderlands Friday \nevening. Rain amounts will be meager, less than a tenth of an \ninch for most areas. A few locations may pick up 0.1-0.2\". \nTemperatures Saturday remain around 15-25 degrees above normal \nas the Northland stays in the warm sector most of the day. Rain \nchance end from west to east Saturday and Saturday night.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH NEXT TUESDAY:\n\nSplit flow aloft will persist into early next week. The \nnorthern stream will transition toward northwest flow as high \npressure drifts eastward across the region. The northwesterly \nflow will bring cooler air aloft into the region. Cooler, near- \nnormal, temperatures are expected Sunday through next Tuesday. \nHighs will be in the mid 40s to low 50s. The high pressure over \nthe region will keep precipitation chances low to zero.",
